<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <!--
        Claude Assistant - stakeholder security groups.

        This addon serves FOUR stakeholder groups. TWO reuse NATIVE Odoo groups
        and are intentionally NOT redefined here:
          - System Administrators  -> base.group_system
          - Business Users         -> base.group_user
        The TWO groups below are the only NEW groups introduced by this addon:
          - Developers             -> claude_assistant.group_developer
          - Partners & Integrators -> claude_assistant.group_partner

        OPERATIONAL RULE (administrative, deliberately NOT enforced via implied_ids):
        group_developer and group_partner must be assigned ONLY to users who
        already hold base.group_user (internal users). This is intentionally NOT
        encoded as implied_ids because the design forbids any implied hierarchy
        among the four stakeholder groups. The only group hierarchy that applies
        is the native chain base.group_system => base.group_erp_manager =>
        base.group_user, which already exists in odoo/addons/base.

        NOTE (Odoo 19.0 schema reality): in this Odoo version res.groups no
        longer exposes a category_id field. The module-category association was
        relocated to the new res.groups.privilege model - res.groups links to it
        through privilege_id, and category_id now lives on res.groups.privilege
        (see odoo/addons/base/models/res_groups_privilege.py and the res.groups
        definition in odoo/addons/base/models/res_groups.py). Writing a
        category_id field on a res.groups record therefore raises
        "ValueError: Invalid field 'category_id' in 'res.groups'" at install.

        Per the design rule "declare ONLY the specified groups/fields - no
        extras" and the scope boundary that forbids any surface area beyond
        these two new groups (i.e. no privilege_id, no res.groups.privilege
        record, no other record), the module-category association is
        intentionally OMITTED here rather than re-introduced via a privilege.
        Category membership is purely a Settings UI grouping concern: it has NO
        effect on res.users.has_group(), on the ir.model.access grants, or on
        the controller's mode authorization. Each group's name is already
        self-describing ("Claude Assistant / <role>"), so with no privilege the
        computed full_name resolves cleanly to that name. This Odoo-core schema
        change is documented here, not worked around, per project policy.
    -->
    <record model="res.groups" id="group_developer">
        <field name="name">Claude Assistant / Developer</field>
    </record>

    <record model="res.groups" id="group_partner">
        <field name="name">Claude Assistant / Partner &amp; Integrator</field>
    </record>
</odoo>
